## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical cache invalidation design across multiple servers and cache backends, including stampedes, per-user invalidation, and pub/sub usage.

## Key Concepts
- Cache-aside pattern
- Per-user key naming and TTL
- Invalidation triggers for writes
- Distributed locks (SETNX/EX) to avoid stampede
- Pub/Sub advantages with Redis vs lack of notifications in Memcached

## Code Example
```javascript
async function getFeed(userId){
  const key = `feed:user:${userId}`
  const cached = await redis.get(key)
  if(cached) return JSON.parse(cached)
  const lock = `lock:feed:${userId}`
  if(await redis.set(lock, '1', 'NX', 'EX', 5)){
    const fresh = await computeFeed(userId)
    await redis.set(key, JSON.stringify(fresh), 'EX', 300)
    await redis.del(lock)
    return fresh
  } else {
    await sleep(50)
    return getFeed(userId)
  }
}

// Invalidation path (triggered by writes)
async function invalidateFeed(userId){
  const key = `feed:user:${userId}`
  await redis.del(key)
  if(usePubSub){
    await redis.publish(`feed-inval:${userId}`, 'invalidate')
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to blend multiple token types, implement a small, pluggable policy engine, and ensure tenant-scoped access with TTL caching. It also touches SAML adapters and middleware integration.

## Key Concepts
- Token normalization, per-tenant policy language, TTL cache
- SAML-to-claims adapter, middleware enforcement
- Edge cases: clock skew, cache invalidation, tenant isolation

## Code Example
```typescript
// Pseudo-implementation sketch
type Claims = { tenant:string; scopes:string[]; sub:string }
function normalize(token:string):Claims { /* validate OIDC/SAML and map to internal claims */ }
function loadPolicy(tenant:string, path:string):Policy|undefined { /* TTL cache */ }
function authorize(token:string, path:string):boolean {
  const c = normalize(token);
  const p = loadPolicy(c.tenant, path);
  return p ? c.scopes.includes(p.requiredScope) && c.tenant===p.tenant : false;
}
```

